==Latin==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*kaput}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*kauput-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*kaput|*káput|head}}. [[cognate|Cognates]] include {{cog|de|Haupt}} and {{cog|en|head}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Classical}} {{IPA|/ˈka.put/|lang=la}}

===Noun===
{{la-noun|caput||capitis|n|third}}

# [[head]]
# {{lb|la|New Latin|anatomy}} headlike [[protuberance]] on an [[organ]] or [[body]] part, usually [[bone]], for instance ''caput [[ulna|ulnae]]''
# {{lb|la|New Latin|medicine}} a [[disease]]; a severe swelling of the soft tissues of a newborn's scalp that develops as the baby travels through the birth canal
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} the [[vital]] part
# {{lb|la|of a river}} [[origin]], [[source]], [[head]]
#* {{Q|la|Horace|Odes|1|1|quote=nunc ad aquae lene '''caput''' sacrae.}}
# {{lb|la|figuratively}} [[life]]
# [[capital city]]
#* {{Q|la|Titus Livius|Ab Urbe Condita|1|16|quote=ut mea Roma '''caput''' orbis terrarum sit}}
#* {{Q|la|Titus Livius|Ab Urbe Condita|8|4|quote=Romam '''caput''' Latio esse}}
# {{lb|la|poetic}} [[leader]], [[chief]]
# {{lb|la|in writings}} {{l|en|division}}, {{l|en|section}}, {{l|en|paragraph}}, {{l|en|chapter}}

====Usage notes====
{{m|la||Caput}} can be used with either a genitive or a dative in the sense of a [[capital city]].

====Inflection====
{{la-decl-3rd-N|caput|capit}}

====Alternative forms====
* {{sense|part or division of a writing}} {{l|la|cap.}}, {{l|la|c.}}

